column124:
12/Dec/20 5:12 PM;557058:f99670ac-6f0b-469b-bdfb-a9817a67932f;On Firefox 83:

* Autocomplete fields for fandom tags offer suggestions as usual on new work form and work search form.
* On a work with lots of comments: the comments button scrolls to and expands the comment section, clicking to the next/previous page of comments from the bottom navigation does not scroll back to the top of the comment section.

I think the problem is with this event handler:

{code:javascript}$j('#comments_placeholder').find('.pagination').on('click.rails', 'a[data-remote]', function(e){
  $j.scrollTo('#comments_placeholder');
});{code}

If the work doesn’t already have the comments section open when initial loaded, {{#comments_placeholder}} will be empty and the handler cannot be attached to {{.pagination}}.

column27:
Originally reported on Google Code with ID 3601
Purpose of code changes:
Some users are wanting to use "chat-style" alternating formatting in their stories,
mimicing the layout of an iPhone or other device. The CSS to do this is notably complex.
Propose adding the following block to the Basic Formatting workskin to make lives easier:


/* section: chat dialogue */
/* to use: use DIV CLASS="chat" around the entire dialogue.
mark each speaker with P CLASS="user1" or P CLASS="user2" as appropriate. */
/* the design is intended to be transformative of a popular chat client without being
derivative. */

#workskin .chat {
  width: 25em;
  margin: 2em auto;
  overflow: auto;
}

#workskin .user1 {
  background-color: #F5F5F5;
  float: left;
  clear: both;
  max-width: 15em;
  border: 1px solid #666;
  -webkit-border-radius: 20px;
  -moz-border-radius: 20px;
  border-radius: 20px;
  border-bottom-left-radius: 0;
  margin: .25em auto;
  padding: .75em;
}

#workskin .user2 {
  background-color: #8DC6FF;
  float: right;
  clear: both;
  max-width: 15em;
  border: 1px solid #666;
  -webkit-border-radius: 20px;
  -moz-border-radius: 20px;
  border-radius: 20px;
  border-bottom-right-radius: 0;
  margin: .25em auto;
  padding: .75em;
}


When reviewing my code changes, please focus on:
cross browser support - I've tested on Chrome, Firefox, IE10 on Windows 7 and stock
and Opera Beta on Android. I know it will degrade in IE8, because IE8 does not border-*-radius...

test work: http://testarchive.transformativeworks.org/works/564543

column124:
19/Jun/14 2:35 PM;557058:acee4fce-21cc-4291-8fa0-d813e774e099;So Bootstrap is on 3.1.1 and we've just upgraded to 2.3.1? :)

As Test was down, I verified that the minified file was indeed the new 2.3.1 on my
webdev by looking at the diff on Github (https://github.com/otwcode/otwarchive/commit/53e34cba52c4203b5cb577554d3affdd34b307bb)
and then searching for the first difference. In the diff, it appears as:

 if (!isActive || (isActive && e.keyCode == 27)) {
         if (e.which == 27) $parent.find(toggle).focus()
         return $this.click()
 }

which minified gives us:

if(!a||a&&n.keyCode==27){if(n.which==27)u.find(t).focus();return r.click()}

The dropdowns work fine in Chrome 35 and IE 11 on Windows (the latter without JS).

Still needs to be confirmed on Test when it's back and preferably also with at least
one mobile device.

column123:
14/Apr/26 10:01 PM;712020:c6679159-6662-4054-9969-6e556eb0bd39;Hi, I've opened a PR for this issue: [https://github.com/otwcode/otwarchive/pull/5734|https://github.com/otwcode/otwarchive/pull/5734|smart-link] 

The fix captures the bookmark IDs before {{update_all}} and then enqueues them to{{IndexQueue}} for reindexing, since {{update_all}}skips ActiveRecord callbacks and the {{after_save :enqueue_to_index}} never fires.

@sarken Let me know how this looks to you.

