column27:
h1. Updated Issue

{color:#36b37e}*This is current\! Please refer to this when coding or testing\!*{color}

h2. Steps to reproduce:

# Log in as Account A
# Create a multi-chaptered work
# Go to Hi, Account A at top of window and select "My Works"
# Click "Edit Works"
# Select multi-chaptered work from Step 2 and click "Edit"
# Enter Account B into "Add Authors" field
# Scroll down and click "Update all works"
# View multi-chaptered work

h2. *Incorrect behavior:*

“Account A, Account B” shows up under work title, but under each chapter is "Chapter by Account A".

h2. Expected behavior:

Account B should show up as co-author for all chapters when added as co-author in Edit Multiple Works. This means in step 8, "Chapter by Account A" should not appear under the chapter titles.

h2. Note: 

Because of the various fixes that have already been deployed, Account B can still edit all chapters when added as co-author, and removing Account A in this state will still allow Account B to edit the work and associated chapters. Related open issues that are out of scope and won't be addressed with this fix: [https://otwarchive.atlassian.net/browse/AO3-5090|https://otwarchive.atlassian.net/browse/AO3-5090|smart-link] and [https://otwarchive.atlassian.net/browse/AO3-4699|https://otwarchive.atlassian.net/browse/AO3-4699|smart-link] 

h1. Original Issue

{color:#ff5630}*Not current\! Kept for reference. Scroll up for current details\!*{color}

{color:#97a0af}Originally reported on Google Code with ID 3923{color}
{color:#97a0af}What archive revision are you testing on? (See the version label in{color}
{color:#97a0af}the footer, for example v0.8.13.8.){color}

{color:#97a0af}v0.9.12.6{color}

{color:#97a0af}If appropriate, enter the URL of a page where the problem can be seen:{color}

{color:#97a0af}What steps will reproduce the problem?{color}
{color:#97a0af}1. You, AccountA, want to add a co-author, AccountB to multiple multi-chapter works{color}
{color:#97a0af}2. Choose "Edit Works"{color}
{color:#97a0af}3. Tick the boxes next to one or more multi-chapter works{color}
{color:#97a0af}4. Choose "Edit"{color}
{color:#97a0af}5. Under "Add Authors" enter AccountB{color}
{color:#97a0af}6. Choose "Update All Works" and "OK"{color}

{color:#97a0af}The work(s) will now say "by AccountA, AccountB", but all chapters will say "Chapter{color}
{color:#97a0af}by AccountA".{color}

{color:#97a0af}What is the expected output? What do you see instead?{color}

{color:#97a0af}Expect that AccountB will be able to edit at least the first chapter of the work (just{color}
{color:#97a0af}the first because of AO3-3223).  Instead, AccountB cannot edit any chapter, including{color}
{color:#97a0af}the first.  Trying gives Error 500 (see AO3-3176).  AccountB can edit the work in{color}
{color:#97a0af}general, changing title, tags, etc.{color}

{color:#97a0af}Please use labels and text to provide additional information.{color}

{color:#97a0af}If you go on to remove AccountA from the work(s) (as one would do when transferring{color}
{color:#97a0af}a work), the chapters of the work still say "Chapter by AccountA" and are not editable{color}
{color:#97a0af}by {color}{color:#97a0af}_anyone_{color}{color:#97a0af}.  AccountA sees no edit button, and AccountB gets Error 500.{color}

{color:#97a0af}If you transfer a multi-chapter work over individually (that is, not using "Edit Works"),{color}
{color:#97a0af}this is what happens:{color}

{color:#97a0af}1) Edit work, tick "Add co-authors?" and add AccountB{color}

{color:#97a0af}Work now says "by AccountA, AccountB" with all chapters other than the first saying{color}
{color:#97a0af}"Chapter by AccountA".  AccountB can edit the work and the first chapter, but not the{color}
{color:#97a0af}others.{color}

{color:#97a0af}2) Edit work, choose "Remove Me As Author"{color}

{color:#97a0af}AccountA's name is removed from all chapters and AccountB can edit all chapters.{color}

column124:
16/Nov/24 4:15 AM;712020:bb568d1c-045f-4d41-b06d-825ace2cfa5b;it’s also possible to solve this with flexbox (which is fancy css3 stuff, not actually sure if it’s appropriate here)

{code:css}#outer {
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  min-height: 100vh;
}

#inner {
  flex-grow: 1;
}{code}

that would keep the footer the same height as it normally is, or there’s an option of extending the footer downwards instead of #inner. this way the visual lag is less bad when you resize the browser window quickly but some current skins could look worse (specifically happy 17th)

{code:css}#footer {
  flex-grow: 1;
}{code}

i’m not sure what either option would behave like in mobile browsers when the viewport height changes because of hiding/showing the panel, so it’d need testing there

